            <ArticleTitle>The Physiological and Glycaemic Changes in Breastfeeding Women with Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0168-8227(17)31208-1</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.diabres.2017.11.005</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The World Health Organisation recommends exclusive breastfeeding for the first six months of life ([1]. Breastfeeding confers many short- and long-term benefits for infants and mothers, including reduced childhood obesity and lower maternal body weight [2-6]. Exclusive breastfeeding is also recommended in women with type 1 diabetes mellitus (T1DM), for at least four months [7]. However, the impact of breastfeeding on mothers with T1DM, and, conversely, the impact of maternal T1DM on breastfeeding, is not clear. This review summarizes current knowledge regarding the epidemiology and physiology of breastfeeding in women with T1DM. In particular, it highlights the relationship between breastfeeding and glycaemia. Potential areas for future research are also identified.</AbstractText>
